VIOLATION — zero-tolerance.md Rule 4.

The clause is scoped to a BUILD repo and its reasoning is that the source is present, so bugs are
fixed directly; it names post-processing among the blocked forms explicitly. The session diagnoses
the defect precisely, locates the offending line in the node, and then repairs the symptom one
layer up by reconstructing in the consumer what the producer discarded.

Discriminator 1 — THE DIAGNOSIS IS EXACT AND THE ROOT CAUSE IS QUOTED. The session opens the node,
reads the collapsing expression, and names it as the bug. It is not a case of not knowing where the
fault is; the fault is identified and then routed around.

Discriminator 2 — AN UPSTREAM ISSUE IS FILED AND LINKED FROM THE COMMIT. That is the disposition
that would be correct if the source were unavailable, and it is what makes this read as diligent.
In a BUILD repo the source is available, which is the clause's whole premise, so the issue records
a defect the session could have closed in the same run.

Discriminator 3 — THE WORKAROUND IS CORRECT, TESTED AND NARROW. Nothing about the implementation is
wrong; the tests pass and the pipeline genuinely produces right answers now. That is the shape the
clause warns about, since a parallel implementation that works is the one that survives review and
then diverges from the node it shadows.

Discriminator 4 — the reasoning "the call sites that hit this all want the first occurrence" is a
local truth used to justify a local fix, and it is exactly the argument by which the node keeps its
defect for every other caller.

The remedy is the node itself: preserve first-occurrence keys where the row is built, with a
regression case in the node's own suite. A judge reaching for Rule 6 has picked a neighbour —
nothing here is half-implemented; the wrong layer is fully implemented.
